From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 05:22:27 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] clutter/frame-clock: Aim a missed update at a later cycle
 with the maximum estimate

When the update for the next presentation can't make it with the
maximum update time estimate, the fixed refresh rate scheduler tries the
minimum estimate. When even that is too late, the update is moved to a
later refresh cycle but stays timed by the minimum estimate, so it runs
as late in that cycle as it possibly can.

That also sends the frame callbacks for the update late. A client
drawing every frame, such as a fullscreen game or video player, then
commits its next frame a few milliseconds after the following
presentation, too late for that cycle as well, and the update is again
put off by a whole cycle at its latest possible time. The client stays
locked to every other refresh cycle. On a 120 Hz built-in panel in a
fixed refresh rate mode it gets 60-70 fps (intervals of 16.7 ms, mixed
with 8.3, 12.5 and 20.8 ms), while the same client in the variable
refresh rate mode, or the first client after the frame clock settled,
runs at 120 fps.

When the minimum estimate is too late too, aim at the later presentation
with the maximum estimate, as the scheduler does when it has no minimum
estimate. The update then runs early in the cycle, the client gets its
frame callback in time to commit before the next presentation, and the
scheduler goes back to dispatching each frame for the next cycle.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c b/clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c
index 12f4c90..215d4bc 100644
--- a/clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c
+++ b/clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c
@@ -1239,12 +1239,26 @@ calculate_next_update_time_us (ClutterFrameClock *frame_clock,
 
               if (next_update_time_us < now_us)
                 {
+                  /* Too late even for the minimum estimate: aim at a later
+                   * presentation using the maximum estimate, as when there
+                   * is no minimum estimate. Updating as late as the minimum
+                   * estimate allows would also send frame callbacks late,
+                   * so a client drawing every frame would commit too late
+                   * again, and keep missing every other refresh cycle.
+                   */
                   next_update_time_us =
-                    mtk_extrapolate_next_interval_boundary (next_update_time_us,
+                    mtk_extrapolate_next_interval_boundary (next_presentation_time_us -
+                                                            max_update_time_estimate_us,
                                                             now_us,
                                                             refresh_interval_us);
                   next_presentation_time_us =
-                    next_update_time_us + min_update_time_estimate_us;
+                    next_update_time_us + max_update_time_estimate_us;
+
+                  CLUTTER_NOTE (FRAME_CLOCK,
+                                "%s update too late for the next presentation, "
+                                "updating in %ld µs",
+                                frame_clock->output_name,
+                                next_update_time_us - now_us);
                 }
 
               if (next_presentation_time_us - max_update_time_estimate_us < now_us)
